WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.369 --> 00:00:02.952
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.900
- Welcome back to another
"Addy Hour" episode.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.030
Of course, it's my pleasure

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:12.270
to be able to continue these conversations

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:13.320
on a lot of different topics,

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.670
which I feel are really important

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:17.030
for so many aspects of our lives.

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:17.910
And today, we're really honored

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:21.080
to be able to jump into a topic
around children and youth.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:22.900
So really think about the next generation.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:23.733
And again,

00:00:23.733 --> 00:00:25.890
something that I think is
very relevant for all of us,

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.400
no matter at what level
we interact with those

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.500
in the generations coming up.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:31.333
So today,

00:00:31.333 --> 00:00:34.260
I'm honored to be able to
welcome Dr. Michele Borba,

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:37.580
who is an internationally
renowned educational psychologist

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:40.020
who has published over 20 books.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:41.347
One of those books includes

00:00:41.347 --> 00:00:43.790
"UnSelfie, Why Empathetic Kids Succeed

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:46.370
In Our All-About-Me World."

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:48.370
Michele is an expert in parenting,

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:50.230
bullying and character development.

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:53.120
And she's spoken to over
one million participants

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:54.780
on five continents.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.300
So someone who has
definitely gotten around

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.450
and just really used
that to inform her work.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.000
She's a regular NBC contributor

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.270
who appears regularly on Today.

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:07.107
She's also been featured
as an expert on "Dateline",

00:01:07.107 --> 00:01:10.217
"The View", "Dr. Phil",
"NBC Nightly News",

00:01:10.217 --> 00:01:12.210
"Fox and Friends", "Dr. Oz",

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:14.630
and "The Early Show", among many others.

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:15.860
She also has a forthcoming book,

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:17.470
which we'll be talking about, as well.

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:19.490
And she lives in Palm Springs, California,

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:20.323
with her husband,

00:01:20.323 --> 00:01:22.890
and is also the mother
of three grown sons.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:25.530
So that's one thing that was
intriguing to me, as well.

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:27.180
Given your work, I would love to hear,

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:29.700
you know, to even meet
your sons at some point,

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.360
but that's a little bit of a tangent,

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:34.150
but again, just want to
welcome Dr. Michele Borba

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:35.301
to the "Addy Hour" podcast.

00:01:35.301 --> 00:01:36.134
- Oh, thank you.

00:01:36.134 --> 00:01:37.100
I have been so looking forward to this,

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:38.684
I'm gonna hire you as my new PR firm.

00:01:38.684 --> 00:01:40.804
Thank you.
(Addy and Michele laughing)

00:01:40.804 --> 00:01:42.484
- What a great intro!
- I would take that up.

00:01:42.484 --> 00:01:44.030
Well, thanks so much for being here.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:45.890
And again, the work
you do is so important.

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:47.427
- Oh, thank you.

00:01:47.427 --> 00:01:49.270
- But as my listeners-
- Likewise, to you.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:50.620
- Oh, I really appreciate that.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:52.430
Definitely appreciate that.

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:53.830
As my listeners know, as well,

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:56.820
one of the things that we really
just value on this podcast

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:58.490
is the opportunity to kind
of peel back the layers

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:01.130
as it were, even as we
bring in so many experts.

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.320
So I did wanna start out just asking you

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:04.760
how you're doing at this moment,

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.710
with everything that's
going on in our world,

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:09.057
especially with the things
that have been unfolding

00:02:09.057 --> 00:02:11.270
in Ukraine and Russia, as well?

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:13.240
- Well, I think I've been
sobbing my way through

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.010
the last three days with Ukraine.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:16.120
I, I just,

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:18.620
the abomination is just extraordinary.

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:20.840
I have friends who adopted children

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.680
from Ukrainian orphanages.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.120
The biggest concern right now

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:26.450
is trying to get ahold of them,

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:28.610
and they're trying to.

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:32.358
There's so many hundreds of
kids under the age of even four,

00:02:32.358 --> 00:02:34.640
that they're trying to figure
out how to get 'em enough food

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:38.660
in order to get them across
a 20 mile, a 20-hour walk.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.650
So that's been really hard,

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:42.810
but you know, when I,

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:43.643
the other thing though,

00:02:43.643 --> 00:02:47.180
I have to keep telling myself
is look to the other side.

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:48.860
Otherwise I get too caught in Putin

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:49.980
and I look to the other side

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:54.260
and I saw images of
hundreds of shoes in Poland

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:57.460
and waiting for kids when
they got off the train,

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:01.400
take shoes or the people
in these other countries

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:02.717
with signs saying,

00:03:02.717 --> 00:03:05.630
"Here I am, I've got a
place for you to stay."

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:07.470
That kind of a thing is just,

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:08.640
it gives you that...

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:09.480
there's that evil,

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.050
then, wow, is there
humanity if we look for it.

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:13.460
- Yeah, I think that's so well said.

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:15.760
We're seeing both sides
of humanity in that way.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.220
And it's, I appreciate
you just sharing that.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:19.290
Honestly, too, on, you know,

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:20.410
how you're navigating that

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:23.610
just to have a good place
for yourself, as well.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:24.620
And I know that's something

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:25.810
that's near and dear to your heart

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:27.420
and something I've heard
you speak about as well,

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:30.800
even just the, and in terms
of compassion fatigue.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:33.060
And I was just curious
about how for any of who,

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.560
I'm sure a lot of people
have heard that term,

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:36.700
but how's that actually
relate to the moment

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:39.360
that we're in, as you shared
so, so honestly about?

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:40.680
- I think we're all feeling it.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:41.530
It's absolutely,

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:43.420
if there was one universal diagnosis,

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:44.477
it would be compassion fatigue.

00:03:44.477 --> 00:03:45.910
And what is it?

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:47.810
It's, we all need relationships.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:49.890
We all crave empathy.

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.460
And when social distancing kicked in

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.240
and we all had to be face-to-face

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.850
and we couldn't see
our family and friends,

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:58.900
that's what rejuvenates us.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:01.320
In the other hand, what happened
was stress starts to build.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:04.410
And when stress keeps building
and uncertainty is there,

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.660
you, where you often
dial your empathy down

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:08.900
and the outcome is compassion fatigue.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.037
So when you see yourself being kind of,

00:04:11.037 --> 00:04:13.150
"Oh, I'm not quite already there,

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:15.600
or your energy level is low",

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:18.420
what I'm really trying to do is read books

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.400
that I could get into and absolutely love.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:21.840
I just finished "The Lincoln Highway"

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.600
and I fell in love with an
eight year old kid named Billy.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.782
My gosh, is that kid resilient.

00:04:26.782 --> 00:04:31.080
Or finding good fun movies
that you can see the good side.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.850
I watch the news content
and try to turn it off

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:35.805
so that I can see a different view.

00:04:35.805 --> 00:04:38.730
And I have been doing a
lot of Zooming with people

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:40.970
that I just really enjoy talking to,

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:42.410
'cause it helps me build up.

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:43.570
I think we all need to do that,

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:45.770
find what works for you,

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:47.580
'cause you have to keep your empathy up

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.180
and that stress level down.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:50.817
- Yeah, yeah. So important.

00:04:50.817 --> 00:04:52.080
And I feel like a lot of ways

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.950
we've been trying to be
creative about that as well.

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:56.310
Some of the topics that
we've even talked about

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:57.750
on this podcast have been around

00:04:57.750 --> 00:04:59.760
just the importance of social bonding

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.160
and how that activates
oxytocin in our brains

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:03.297
and all those pieces.

00:05:03.297 --> 00:05:06.000
And you know, whether that's
still there on Zoom or not,

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:07.310
but there's just a different energy

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:08.874
that comes from being in person.

00:05:08.874 --> 00:05:09.871
But at the same time, like you said,

00:05:09.871 --> 00:05:11.820
there are opportunities to be
able to connect with people

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:14.070
in ways that we hadn't done that before.

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:15.410
So appreciate you sharing

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:17.960
how you're navigating
that journey, as well.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:19.590
- We just had to put
our creativity into it.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:21.670
Well, that didn't work. So
what are we gonna do instead?

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:24.293
And if we keep, just keep
looking for the other options,

00:05:24.293 --> 00:05:26.350
I think that's how we thrive.

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:27.750
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:29.440
And thrive, I'm glad you used that word,

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:30.975
'cause that's something we'll
definitely come back to.

00:05:30.975 --> 00:05:31.808
(laughs)

00:05:31.808 --> 00:05:33.280
Maybe that was an intentional, maybe not,

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:35.031
but as we think about
your new book, as well.

00:05:35.031 --> 00:05:35.864
- No, it just is part of my,

00:05:35.864 --> 00:05:37.830
this part of my vocabulary these days,

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:39.020
and what are we gonna do

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:41.200
to keep on doing and raising thrivers?

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:42.940
- Yeah. So important.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:43.890
Before we jump there,

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:46.910
I just also wanted to hear a
little bit about your journey

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:48.437
for those who may be familiar with you

00:05:48.437 --> 00:05:49.960
and those who aren't familiar to you,

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:50.820
how did you,

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:52.590
what started this passion

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:54.680
to get into this area of child development

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.060
in so many directions
that you've taken it?

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:58.220
- Absolute fluke.

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:00.160
I was a, I was a history major.

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:01.491
- Wow.
- And I came home to-

00:06:01.491 --> 00:06:02.917
- I didn't expect that. (laughs)

00:06:02.917 --> 00:06:05.670
- No, I came home to interview my dad

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:08.330
or just be with my dad
from college for a weekend.

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:09.390
And I'd never forget it.

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:10.760
You will appreciate this.

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:12.640
My dad was calm. He was cool.

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:14.290
You know, the world war II veteran,

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:16.310
the superintendent, he published books.

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:19.390
He was quite accomplished,

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:21.330
but he was so upset that day.

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:23.340
And he was holding a Newsweek magazine

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.450
and he was pacing the floor.

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:28.090
He looked at me and he
didn't expect my visit.

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:30.180
And the cover of the magazine

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:32.280
was "The First Three
Years of a Child's Life,

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:33.570
Make or Break Them."

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:35.757
He turned to me, pointed to it and said,

00:06:35.757 --> 00:06:37.320
"Don't buy into this, Michele,

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:39.640
because if this were
true, I'd be dead today."

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:41.720
Now I sat there with my mouth open

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:44.850
because I didn't know anything
about my dad's parents.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:47.430
The had passed away. I
never met my grandparents.

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:49.047
He sat me down and he said,

00:06:49.047 --> 00:06:50.900
"This is what happened to me."

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:53.559
His parents apparently
came over very destitute,

00:06:53.559 --> 00:06:55.660
no English from Italy,

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:58.180
tried desperately to be able to jumpstart

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:00.900
and wanted to give their
kids the best they could.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:02.734
My dad was the youngest of five,

00:07:02.734 --> 00:07:06.490
but my father, grandfather
went and became a coal miner

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:08.360
and then died when my dad was two.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:09.193
- [Addy] Wow.

00:07:09.193 --> 00:07:11.560
- And the only way that
his extremely loving mother

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:14.520
could afford the children
and keep them alive

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:15.845
was to put them in an orphanage.

00:07:15.845 --> 00:07:16.678
- Hmm.

00:07:16.678 --> 00:07:17.511
- So he said, my dad said,

00:07:17.511 --> 00:07:21.030
"Okay, now I'm in an
orphanage. My mom is destitute.

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:22.500
I didn't know what was going on."

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:24.730
But he said, "It was
the people who saved me.

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:27.180
The nuns had such empathy for me, Michele.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:28.860
That was the first thing, those nuns.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:31.110
When they released me, my
mom got a little more money.

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:34.200
It was the local librarian
and she'd sneak books to me.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:35.757
Danny would read this.

00:07:35.757 --> 00:07:38.310
The neighbor next door would help me rake

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:40.300
and give me a little bit extra money.

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:42.320
A teacher would pull me aside and say,

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:44.110
'I think you could be a good writer.'"

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:45.730
He said, "My proudest moment was

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:48.970
when I got my scholarship
from Stanford and UC Berkeley,

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:51.340
to walk in and say, 'thank you very much'.

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:53.320
Don't buy into it, Michele.

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:54.960
People can make or break your life."

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.780
I went back and changed my
major to child development.

00:07:57.780 --> 00:07:59.000
- Wow.
- So I was there,

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:02.590
found this amazing piece
of research by Emmy Warner.

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:04.360
And that started my whole career.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:06.710
Emmy Warner substantiated
what my dad said.

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:09.080
She started to interview,

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:12.240
oh gosh, 40, just for 40 years,

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:14.420
she studied the same 700 children

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:15.890
who shouldn't have made it.

00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:18.300
Extreme adversity in their life.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:20.000
But one-third of them,

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.470
even by their teens
years was bouncing back

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:24.380
and she discovered the same thing,

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:26.330
caring people in their lives,

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:28.110
that would what my dad said.

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:30.530
And then somebody had taught
them protective buffers

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:32.430
or coping strategies.

00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:33.760
That is a result they made it,

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:36.920
and that changed my whole career. Okay.

00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:37.753
- Wow, wow.

00:08:37.753 --> 00:08:38.592
- That is true.

00:08:38.592 --> 00:08:40.250
That we gotta get this
information out to people.

00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:41.800
- Yeah. That's such a powerful story

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:45.170
and I love that it has a
family connection as well.

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:47.010
And it sounds like just in that moment,

00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:49.390
there was a shift and
a passion that emerged.

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:51.070
- Oh, it was an amazing shift.

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:51.927
And later on, he said,

00:08:51.927 --> 00:08:53.820
"Michele, you should be
a special ed teacher.

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:55.260
That's how I ended up in special ed.

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:59.533
Everything I learned from every
part of my so-called career

00:08:59.533 --> 00:09:00.808
was from kids.

00:09:00.808 --> 00:09:02.690
They, it wasn't the textbook,

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:04.007
it was the kids who convinced me,

00:09:04.007 --> 00:09:06.020
boy, this is doable stuff.

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:08.570
- Yeah. The hands-on,
real life experience.

00:09:08.570 --> 00:09:09.720
Wow. Wow.

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:10.750
Definitely appreciate that.

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:11.720
And as you were,

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:14.350
as you were getting into the work,

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:16.267
how did do people respond
to you, were people...

00:09:16.267 --> 00:09:17.460
'Cause in the same way,

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:19.170
it seems like there could
be a mixed reaction,

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:21.450
that either people would
be really excited about it

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:23.210
or did people ask you why,

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:25.160
why this route?

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:25.993
- Why this route?

00:09:25.993 --> 00:09:27.230
You know, interestingly enough,

00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:28.420
you're probably one of the few people

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:30.591
that asked why this route,
(Addy and Michele laughing)

00:09:30.591 --> 00:09:31.424
so thank you very much.

00:09:31.424 --> 00:09:32.879
I, my goal is...

00:09:32.879 --> 00:09:37.080
Funny, I just took a very
slow path of a trajectory.

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:39.570
Whenever a phone call
would ring or something,

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:41.270
I went into a different director.

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:43.900
I got my masters in learning disabilities.

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:47.680
And then, I found this passion
for educational psychology.

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:49.470
That's where I got my doctorate.

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:51.297
I never planned to write a book,

00:09:51.297 --> 00:09:54.350
but I had one of my
professors tell me I should.

00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:55.790
And then once I wrote the book,

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:57.260
I never planned to be a speaker,

00:09:57.260 --> 00:09:59.360
but somebody called up and
said, "We need you to speak."

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:01.340
So it was saying yes

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:03.990
to all of these little
avenues along the way

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:05.331
and I'm so glad I did.

00:10:05.331 --> 00:10:07.810
- Yeah. And it seems like
it's tapped into your passion

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:08.770
that was already there too.

00:10:08.770 --> 00:10:10.050
And so in a sense,

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:12.100
you were poised and ready for
the opportunities that came

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:13.700
because you're already
following that passion,

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:15.277
which I think is really
encouraging to hear.

00:10:15.277 --> 00:10:18.010
- Well, I have no idea
on how critical it is

00:10:18.010 --> 00:10:19.740
to find that passion, what's your joy,

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:22.550
what you just absolutely love to do.

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:25.820
And just helping kids and
working with families,

00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:28.590
no matter where I am is my passion.

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:32.346
And I had the absolute,
amazing opportunity to work on.

00:10:32.346 --> 00:10:34.610
I think five continents now.

00:10:34.610 --> 00:10:36.580
I'm up to 23 countries.
- Wow.

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:40.040
- And what I've learned from
it is no matter where you are,

00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:42.810
everybody has the same loves and concerns

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:45.080
and desires for their kids.

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:47.660
So it works no matter where we are,

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:50.170
let's all work together,
we're just all neighbors.

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:51.178
- Yeah. That's so important,

00:10:51.178 --> 00:10:53.016
especially with everything we've been...

00:10:53.016 --> 00:10:53.849
- Yeah.

00:10:53.849 --> 00:10:54.790
- Walking through in
the last few years too,

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:56.762
would you say that, was
that a surprise to you?

00:10:56.762 --> 00:10:57.810
Or do you think that you expected to,

00:10:57.810 --> 00:10:59.403
to see that commonality?

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:05.030
- Well, travel has broken
every stereotype I had of life.

00:11:05.030 --> 00:11:07.578
If we could all travel more,

00:11:07.578 --> 00:11:10.970
it helps you, no matter where you are,

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:13.180
just be far more open

00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:15.594
and realize they're just like
me, no matter where I am,

00:11:15.594 --> 00:11:17.245
I don't care if it's religion, education,

00:11:17.245 --> 00:11:19.560
you know, race, color, creed.

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:21.400
Oh, my gosh. People are wonderful,

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:23.270
with my best friends, I've
got them all over the world.

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:26.140
And I just got another call
from Abu Dhabi this morning

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:27.260
and said, "Can you come back?"

00:11:27.260 --> 00:11:28.670
Oh, I'd love to come back.

00:11:28.670 --> 00:11:29.800
- Wow.
It's just so fun.

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:30.980
- Yeah. That's so powerful.

00:11:30.980 --> 00:11:32.120
I mean, it speaks to this,

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:34.070
the global community that
we have in the sense, too.

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:36.540
And even as you were sharing
about your dad's story,

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:37.750
just making me think about the community

00:11:37.750 --> 00:11:39.170
that he had around him,

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:41.470
all those people who
are sewn into his life

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:43.830
in different ways and giving
him encouragement, the empathy,

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:46.300
the empowerment, I think
that's so critical,

00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:47.240
and so encouraging to hear

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:49.740
that the way that you've
built that into your work,

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:52.420
even as one individual who has
traveled to all these places

00:11:52.420 --> 00:11:53.820
and has learned so much

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:55.890
and is really sharing
that with so many of us.

00:11:55.890 --> 00:11:58.760
- I love Susan Pinker's
book, "The Village Effect"

00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:01.860
because she said, if you
really wanna boost empathy,

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:05.520
which is to me, the social glue
that holds us all together,

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:08.300
remember that villages of about 150 people

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:11.160
are the maximum of what kids really need

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:13.044
in order to create that community.

00:12:13.044 --> 00:12:13.940
And what we've done is,

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:16.000
we've busted the villages

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:18.920
and we're these huge,
huge, suburban areas.

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:20.585
One of my favorite things to do is,

00:12:20.585 --> 00:12:22.490
as I'm driving from, I don't care,

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:24.610
if it's Sri Lanka or New York,

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:26.440
I purposely go through an area

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:29.070
and I can go from a rural area,

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:31.380
drive through and I watch
the facial expressions

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:32.830
and they look like they're happy,

00:12:32.830 --> 00:12:33.750
and the all of a sudden,

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:36.200
as I go into a bigger
area, in an urban area,

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:38.050
a little bit more stress comes in.

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:41.560
So maybe the first thing is we
just gotta capture each other

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:43.610
and realize we are the support system.

00:12:43.610 --> 00:12:45.240
Let's get back to what we all need

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:47.010
for optimum mental health.

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:48.180
And as parents,

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:50.410
let's also look at what we're modeling.

00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:52.760
Are we modeling thinking outside the box,

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:54.960
exposing our children to differences,

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:56.800
letting them be exposed

00:12:56.800 --> 00:12:59.662
to how many wonderful other
opportunities they are.

00:12:59.662 --> 00:13:01.310
You know, there's a great study

00:13:01.310 --> 00:13:03.460
that also said, during the pandemic,

00:13:03.460 --> 00:13:06.060
the kids who seem to fare a little better

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:08.930
were the kids who didn't
take the bus from New York

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:09.870
to their school,

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:12.480
but walked through the park in
order to get to their school.

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:15.350
Just those simple, ordinary things.

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:16.240
I'll mention one thing

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:17.710
when I was writing on resilience,

00:13:17.710 --> 00:13:20.950
I've been trying to study this
for 40 years after my dad.

00:13:20.950 --> 00:13:24.360
It's one of the best books I
also read was by Anne Maston.

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:26.470
She's from the University of Minnesota.

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:28.600
And what she did was she discovered

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:29.900
when she did the same thing

00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:32.670
of kids growing up in extreme adversity,

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:34.220
is that ordinary things

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:37.620
can make extraordinary
differences on children's lives.

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:38.453
And when I looked at,

00:13:38.453 --> 00:13:40.150
what do you mean by the ordinary things?

00:13:40.150 --> 00:13:42.010
Like the walk through the park,

00:13:42.010 --> 00:13:45.070
like prayer, like a sense of humor,

00:13:45.070 --> 00:13:46.500
like a, some kind of a,

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:49.660
a talent or a hobby or an interest,

00:13:49.660 --> 00:13:51.910
knowing I've got one friend.

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:53.100
And I looked at that and said,

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:54.710
that's what seems to be a problem,

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:56.847
what we've got is ordinary things

00:13:56.847 --> 00:13:59.560
being removed a lot from
our children's lives

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:01.610
and maybe we just need to reboot.

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:02.537
- [Addy] Yeah.

00:14:02.537 --> 00:14:04.620
- And it's not just the GPA

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:06.530
that's gonna help our kids in this world.

00:14:06.530 --> 00:14:09.350
It's those ordinary
things that help fill them

00:14:09.350 --> 00:14:11.830
and are going to be
really good decompressors

00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:14.870
to help them get along with life.

00:14:14.870 --> 00:14:15.810
- Yeah.

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:18.360
So many things that you
said that I'd love to unpack

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:19.193
and I'll pick a few,

00:14:19.193 --> 00:14:21.020
but even as you're talking
about those ordinary things,

00:14:21.020 --> 00:14:22.790
those small things, I
mean, as a neuroscientist,

00:14:22.790 --> 00:14:25.510
even thinking about what we
understand about the brain

00:14:25.510 --> 00:14:26.343
and having the brace

00:14:26.343 --> 00:14:28.010
and just the things in our environment

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:29.070
that can bring us pleasure.

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:32.640
And even as we can engage
in our day-to-day activity,

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:34.590
talking that really can help us navigate

00:14:34.590 --> 00:14:37.160
and just have a place of
mental health and wellness.

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:40.030
And I think oftentimes, we talk
about that in adult circles,

00:14:40.030 --> 00:14:40.910
we don't always,

00:14:40.910 --> 00:14:42.700
I think as much as we need to,

00:14:42.700 --> 00:14:45.160
really think about that
for children and youth,

00:14:45.160 --> 00:14:46.560
where it's even that much more important,

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:48.740
especially from a
developmental standpoint.

00:14:48.740 --> 00:14:49.573
So it's great.

00:14:49.573 --> 00:14:50.587
Great that you've been
paying attention to that

00:14:50.587 --> 00:14:52.410
and to elevate that as well.

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:53.243
- Oh, you know,

00:14:53.243 --> 00:14:55.020
one of the things that when you asked,

00:14:55.020 --> 00:14:56.790
what am I looking forward to right now

00:14:56.790 --> 00:14:59.250
is I'm finally being able
to get back on the road

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:00.300
and go and visit schools.

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:02.720
And the best thing I get to do

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:04.710
before I talk to the big school district

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:06.160
is do focus groups with teens.

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:07.830
- [Addy] Mmm.
- Oh, my gosh.

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:09.810
They just are so extraordinary.

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:12.020
Once they know whatever they say,

00:15:12.020 --> 00:15:14.010
it's gonna be, there's no judgment,

00:15:14.010 --> 00:15:15.040
just tell me.

00:15:15.040 --> 00:15:16.930
So I've been trying to
figure out and ask them,

00:15:16.930 --> 00:15:19.002
you know, what's causing
all of this stress

00:15:19.002 --> 00:15:21.570
and what are the solutions for it?

00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:25.650
And one kid, I just came
from college station, Texas.

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:28.050
He said, "We are so stressed.

00:15:28.050 --> 00:15:31.340
But the thing is that nobody's
teaching us how to de-stress

00:15:31.340 --> 00:15:33.330
and it doesn't,

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:35.200
isn't something you learn on a worksheet

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:37.630
and it's not like a one time health unit.

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:39.010
The big thing you guys,

00:15:39.010 --> 00:15:40.370
you guys have gotta do,

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:43.030
is give us a repertoire of stuff,

00:15:43.030 --> 00:15:45.340
and then we choose what works for us.

00:15:45.340 --> 00:15:47.520
And then we have got to practice that

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:49.080
over and over and over and over

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:50.163
until we can do it without you.

00:15:50.163 --> 00:15:53.352
I'm like, "Oh, my gosh,
textbook classic 101."

00:15:53.352 --> 00:15:54.185
- That's so insightful.

00:15:54.185 --> 00:15:55.018
(Addy and Michele laughing)

00:15:55.018 --> 00:15:55.851
- I said,

00:15:55.851 --> 00:15:56.760
so if you're gonna make us
do that deep breathing stuff,

00:15:56.760 --> 00:15:57.690
that's cool.

00:15:57.690 --> 00:16:00.140
But don't just tell us what it is,

00:16:00.140 --> 00:16:01.160
every single day,

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:03.138
we should practice that
maybe two minutes a day.

00:16:03.138 --> 00:16:04.460
- Mmm.

00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:05.293
Yeah.

00:16:05.293 --> 00:16:07.450
- That's exactly, we can
be doing that at home.

00:16:07.450 --> 00:16:08.810
When we say we're so tired,

00:16:08.810 --> 00:16:10.120
no, get two minutes a day.

00:16:10.120 --> 00:16:14.810
Just practice one thing until
it becomes a new family habit.

00:16:14.810 --> 00:16:16.130
I say that one

00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:18.110
because one of the coolest
places I ever worked

00:16:18.110 --> 00:16:19.812
was on army basis.

00:16:19.812 --> 00:16:22.640
18 of them, and the
commanders kept telling me,

00:16:22.640 --> 00:16:24.200
you should go talk to the Navy Seals.

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:25.033
I said, why.

00:16:25.033 --> 00:16:26.960
He says it's because
we are retraining them

00:16:26.960 --> 00:16:29.570
to get through some really
tough, adverse times

00:16:29.570 --> 00:16:31.470
and I'm going, "That's for sure."

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:33.157
So when I asked the Navy Seal's I said,

00:16:33.157 --> 00:16:33.990
"What are you guys doing?"

00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:35.450
They said, "Three things,

00:16:35.450 --> 00:16:36.660
and you better tell kids this,

00:16:36.660 --> 00:16:39.120
'cause this is really helping us

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:41.697
and it's actually proven
to rewire our brains."

00:16:41.697 --> 00:16:42.997
"Okay, what's one?"

00:16:42.997 --> 00:16:45.000
"We figure out what our stress signs are

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:46.680
and we start pointing
it out to each other.

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:48.480
We don't wait till the meltdown.

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:49.520
We point 'em out.

00:16:49.520 --> 00:16:50.970
Hey, you start grinding your teeth

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:52.230
or you start going like this,

00:16:52.230 --> 00:16:53.350
or I can see, you know,

00:16:53.350 --> 00:16:55.620
your heart hopping up and down.

00:16:55.620 --> 00:16:57.310
We could do that to our kids.

00:16:57.310 --> 00:16:59.110
Not that you're gonna get time out,

00:16:59.110 --> 00:17:00.691
but just have you noticed,

00:17:00.691 --> 00:17:02.860
that right before you
go to do the time test,

00:17:02.860 --> 00:17:04.250
this is what you do."

00:17:04.250 --> 00:17:07.720
Then they say, "We tell
ourself, chill out or relax.

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:09.560
And then we take a one-two breath,

00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:13.350
slow two breath from our
abdomen, nothing new,

00:17:13.350 --> 00:17:14.960
but it's slow two breath,

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:17.280
way like we're riding up an elevator,

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:18.530
keep focusing on the breath,

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:21.210
hold it and then slowly let it out.

00:17:21.210 --> 00:17:23.310
So the exhale's twice
as long as the inhale.

00:17:23.310 --> 00:17:24.490
And you see Berkeley says,

00:17:24.490 --> 00:17:25.970
that's exactly what works.

00:17:25.970 --> 00:17:26.803
- [Addy] Yeah.

00:17:26.803 --> 00:17:28.160
- Why do we teach that to kids?

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:29.250
Doesn't cost a dime.

00:17:29.250 --> 00:17:30.530
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:17:30.530 --> 00:17:31.630
So important. And on that note,

00:17:31.630 --> 00:17:33.020
I'm curious because even as you're talking

00:17:33.020 --> 00:17:34.830
about the focus groups with the teens,

00:17:34.830 --> 00:17:36.480
it seems like there's a push and pull.

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:37.910
I mean, even the, you know,

00:17:37.910 --> 00:17:40.220
the kids that I've talked to in schools

00:17:40.220 --> 00:17:41.230
and having kids myself too,

00:17:41.230 --> 00:17:43.270
it seems like sometimes
there's just different places

00:17:43.270 --> 00:17:44.650
that people are at.

00:17:44.650 --> 00:17:46.470
So I'm curious what your view is of

00:17:46.470 --> 00:17:48.850
where those mental health
conversations are for kids.

00:17:48.850 --> 00:17:50.700
'Cause sometimes I get the sense

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:51.850
that the kids are thinking about it

00:17:51.850 --> 00:17:53.940
more than the administrators
or the teachers,

00:17:53.940 --> 00:17:55.280
and now to downplay the efforts

00:17:55.280 --> 00:17:57.180
that the administrators and
teachers are putting in,

00:17:57.180 --> 00:17:59.550
but seems like sometimes
there's a disconnect,

00:17:59.550 --> 00:18:03.120
almost as if kids in youth
are more ready to move into it

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:04.460
than we give them credit for.

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:06.173
Now, I guess I'm answering
the question as I'm saying it,

00:18:06.173 --> 00:18:07.912
but I'm curious if that's
been your perception.

00:18:07.912 --> 00:18:08.745
- Oh, no,

00:18:08.745 --> 00:18:10.120
I knew we would agree on anything

00:18:10.120 --> 00:18:12.250
that comes outta your mouth
is gonna come outta mine.

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:13.083
And I think that's,

00:18:13.083 --> 00:18:15.990
what's happening right now
is the pandemic aftermath.

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:19.850
We knew that it was going to
be a stressful for us all,

00:18:19.850 --> 00:18:21.180
but now when we're looking at,

00:18:21.180 --> 00:18:23.020
okay, now we can get back to normal.

00:18:23.020 --> 00:18:24.700
I think we ought to put the red flag up

00:18:24.700 --> 00:18:27.520
and go, "Really? What's
going on with the kids?"

00:18:27.520 --> 00:18:30.210
The first thing that the kids
are saying that I had seen,

00:18:30.210 --> 00:18:31.840
but they're now admitting,

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:33.320
their motivation is lower.

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:35.010
Their focusing abilities are lower

00:18:35.010 --> 00:18:37.250
and this is the top performing kids.

00:18:37.250 --> 00:18:39.340
These are, we're more irritable.

00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:41.080
We can't handle the stress.

00:18:41.080 --> 00:18:43.550
We're not sleeping as well as we used to.

00:18:43.550 --> 00:18:45.160
So you put all that together,

00:18:45.160 --> 00:18:46.700
the last thing you're
gonna be able to do is

00:18:46.700 --> 00:18:48.630
they sit there for three
hours and keep going.

00:18:48.630 --> 00:18:51.670
They said, you gotta
reinvent a little bit.

00:18:51.670 --> 00:18:55.100
That homework load are
just astronomical on us.

00:18:55.100 --> 00:18:57.743
We really are having a tough time

00:18:57.743 --> 00:19:00.500
and we really need to know,

00:19:00.500 --> 00:19:03.280
feel comfortable going in
talking to the counselors.

00:19:03.280 --> 00:19:05.140
Many of the counselors have been,

00:19:05.140 --> 00:19:06.910
they're wonderful, they said,

00:19:06.910 --> 00:19:07.743
that they've been,

00:19:07.743 --> 00:19:10.540
they've been really helping
us prior to coming in

00:19:10.540 --> 00:19:13.470
with our GPAs and our getting to college.

00:19:13.470 --> 00:19:14.303
- Mm.

00:19:14.303 --> 00:19:18.620
- You want us to now open up
and tell them how upset we are

00:19:18.620 --> 00:19:20.500
or how much in pain we are.

00:19:20.500 --> 00:19:22.540
We got to build trust.
- Mmm hmm.

00:19:22.540 --> 00:19:24.970
- I love the comments they came up with

00:19:24.970 --> 00:19:26.340
because they're just, you know,

00:19:26.340 --> 00:19:27.890
you could read any psychology book

00:19:27.890 --> 00:19:29.139
and it's exactly the same thing.

00:19:29.139 --> 00:19:30.033
- Exactly. Yeah, yeah.

00:19:30.033 --> 00:19:31.380
- I think the first thing is

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:34.150
we are seeing a regression
in behavior patterns.

00:19:34.150 --> 00:19:38.207
If I was to ask any teacher
or counselor or headmaster,

00:19:38.207 --> 00:19:39.580
"What are you seeing?"

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:40.600
Kid regression,

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:43.270
more disrespect is coming up,

00:19:43.270 --> 00:19:45.800
more, the whole society is disrespectful.

00:19:45.800 --> 00:19:47.470
The adults have been behaving very badly.

00:19:47.470 --> 00:19:49.097
I think it's spilling over to the kids.

00:19:49.097 --> 00:19:52.619
But the second thing
is inability to really,

00:19:52.619 --> 00:19:55.677
that motivation and the
perseverance isn't there.

00:19:55.677 --> 00:19:57.060
But in all fairness,

00:19:57.060 --> 00:19:58.620
you can't have the perseverance

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:00.360
unless you have the self-control.

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:01.770
Unless you have the confidence

00:20:01.770 --> 00:20:04.360
and they all kind of work
together, as you know, so...

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:06.680
You have to start with where the kids are.

00:20:06.680 --> 00:20:08.600
- Yeah. I think that's so, so well said.

00:20:08.600 --> 00:20:10.670
One of the episodes we had recently was,

00:20:10.670 --> 00:20:11.680
again, more on the counsel,

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:13.970
but some one who's an expert
in trauma informed teaching

00:20:13.970 --> 00:20:14.990
and learning too.

00:20:14.990 --> 00:20:16.820
And just acknowledging all the things

00:20:16.820 --> 00:20:18.610
that the kids have gone
through and how, like you said,

00:20:18.610 --> 00:20:21.590
that's going to affect their
actions in the classroom,

00:20:21.590 --> 00:20:22.540
but not ignoring that,

00:20:22.540 --> 00:20:23.610
and trying to just push beyond

00:20:23.610 --> 00:20:25.530
about being aware of that too.

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:27.370
What do you say to parents
and teachers though?

00:20:27.370 --> 00:20:30.140
'Cause I know sometimes the
comments that comes back,

00:20:30.140 --> 00:20:31.297
the comments that come back, people say,

00:20:31.297 --> 00:20:34.310
"Well, I'm here to teach.
I'm not a therapist.

00:20:34.310 --> 00:20:35.890
I don't know how to navigate this."

00:20:35.890 --> 00:20:37.010
And so even though,

00:20:37.010 --> 00:20:38.130
as you're saying appropriately,

00:20:38.130 --> 00:20:40.770
we need to push beyond just the GPA piece.

00:20:40.770 --> 00:20:42.530
Sometimes people find that very difficult

00:20:42.530 --> 00:20:43.680
to break outside of that.

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:45.134
Do you have tips that you try and share?

00:20:45.134 --> 00:20:46.443
- Yeah, the most important thing

00:20:46.443 --> 00:20:47.276
- In situations?

00:20:47.276 --> 00:20:49.100
- For instance, I'm going
to Indiana tomorrow.

00:20:49.100 --> 00:20:50.700
And what I'm trying to do is

00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:52.210
when I talk to the teachers,

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:54.580
it's all about, I know you're teaching,

00:20:54.580 --> 00:20:56.060
don't stop the lesson,

00:20:56.060 --> 00:20:59.150
but let's find simple little
ways to weave in these things.

00:20:59.150 --> 00:21:01.330
For instance, as you're talking,

00:21:01.330 --> 00:21:03.090
you know that your kid's attention spans,

00:21:03.090 --> 00:21:05.680
I don't care if they're
17 or three are shorter.

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:07.020
So take brain breaks.

00:21:07.020 --> 00:21:09.190
And that is don't talk for 50 minutes,

00:21:09.190 --> 00:21:11.140
instead, let's talk for 10 minutes

00:21:11.140 --> 00:21:13.770
and then let's take a
30-second turn to your partner,

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:15.260
what's one thing you just heard?

00:21:15.260 --> 00:21:17.560
You're gonna get more
retention from your kids

00:21:17.560 --> 00:21:18.970
or just pick up your pencil

00:21:18.970 --> 00:21:21.630
and draw one little thing
that the teacher just said,

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:22.720
that's one thing.

00:21:22.720 --> 00:21:25.270
One of the best things I've
ever seen is quiet time

00:21:25.270 --> 00:21:27.200
that's being done in the Oakland schools.

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:30.160
What they did brilliantly was shave off

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:31.630
one minute of each class time,

00:21:31.630 --> 00:21:33.260
just one minute of each class time.

00:21:33.260 --> 00:21:34.480
Now, you have five minutes

00:21:34.480 --> 00:21:36.550
at the beginning of every single day,

00:21:36.550 --> 00:21:39.610
five minutes at the beginning
of every single day,

00:21:39.610 --> 00:21:41.100
every kid in the entire school

00:21:41.100 --> 00:21:43.483
and the teachers do one-two breathing.

00:21:44.440 --> 00:21:45.890
You can wedge if you want to,

00:21:45.890 --> 00:21:47.752
half the kids are sleeping
for the first time.

00:21:47.752 --> 00:21:50.860
You can choose meditation
if you choose to.

00:21:50.860 --> 00:21:52.570
But here's the fabulous thing,

00:21:52.570 --> 00:21:54.030
they've been tracking it.

00:21:54.030 --> 00:21:55.180
Parties are going down.

00:21:55.180 --> 00:21:56.510
Suspensions are going down.

00:21:56.510 --> 00:21:57.860
Behavior problems are going down.

00:21:57.860 --> 00:21:59.640
Test scores went through the roof.

00:21:59.640 --> 00:22:01.410
They did absolutely nothing different

00:22:01.410 --> 00:22:05.260
than let's just focus on
what the kids seem to need.

00:22:05.260 --> 00:22:06.850
Let's teach them how,

00:22:06.850 --> 00:22:08.450
let's have a counselor come in

00:22:08.450 --> 00:22:10.750
and show you how to do a one-two breath.

00:22:10.750 --> 00:22:13.070
And it made mind-boggling
like differences.

00:22:13.070 --> 00:22:15.690
So it's not that you're stopping teaching,

00:22:15.690 --> 00:22:16.523
- Exactly.

00:22:16.523 --> 00:22:19.470
- It's that you're going
with where your students are,

00:22:19.470 --> 00:22:22.000
trying to get them more
engaged because they're not.

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:23.440
They're also, by the way,

00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:24.930
more risk adverse,

00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:26.716
they're afraid to raise their hands.

00:22:26.716 --> 00:22:29.457
They're really, social
anxieties up with our kids

00:22:29.457 --> 00:22:31.848
'cause they've been facing
a screen for so long.

00:22:31.848 --> 00:22:34.210
So a simple thing I also tell a teacher,

00:22:34.210 --> 00:22:35.740
you can tell 'em at home.

00:22:35.740 --> 00:22:39.840
First thing is Mary Budd
Rowe, brilliant researcher.

00:22:39.840 --> 00:22:41.740
She said, you wanna ask a question,

00:22:41.740 --> 00:22:42.890
ask the question,

00:22:42.890 --> 00:22:44.400
then stop and pause,

00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:46.434
and don't say anything for
at least three seconds.

00:22:46.434 --> 00:22:48.790
'Cause the average one of us
says something immediately,

00:22:48.790 --> 00:22:50.050
so what's the answer?

00:22:50.050 --> 00:22:52.020
If you wait three seconds,

00:22:52.020 --> 00:22:54.250
you'll get every kid to say something.

00:22:54.250 --> 00:22:55.460
The kid who wouldn't have said anything

00:22:55.460 --> 00:22:56.800
is gonna open his mouth and say,

00:22:56.800 --> 00:22:58.060
you know, maybe a word.

00:22:58.060 --> 00:22:59.520
The other kid who would've said something

00:22:59.520 --> 00:23:01.090
is now gonna give you a dissertation,

00:23:01.090 --> 00:23:04.760
but it's time to wait and
think can be critical.

00:23:04.760 --> 00:23:06.680
And the final thing is our expectations.

00:23:06.680 --> 00:23:09.350
Parents, it's like a rubber band.

00:23:09.350 --> 00:23:11.960
Your goal was to figure out
what your kid is capable of,

00:23:11.960 --> 00:23:12.793
right this minute.

00:23:12.793 --> 00:23:14.960
If you don't know, ask the teacher,

00:23:14.960 --> 00:23:16.640
but line your expectations up

00:23:16.640 --> 00:23:20.350
and then gently keep
stretching him, gently.

00:23:20.350 --> 00:23:22.450
Don't snap him 'cause
you'll snap his spirit,

00:23:22.450 --> 00:23:24.810
but you keep stretching one step more

00:23:24.810 --> 00:23:26.970
and one step more and one step more.

00:23:26.970 --> 00:23:29.860
Just little things that's
using the research,

00:23:29.860 --> 00:23:32.350
that don't mean it's another program,

00:23:32.350 --> 00:23:33.720
but it means we can make a difference

00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:35.510
on how our kids are more engaged,

00:23:35.510 --> 00:23:37.610
better performers, better behavers

00:23:37.610 --> 00:23:40.140
and their confidence will also go up.

00:23:40.140 --> 00:23:41.820
- Yeah, that's so powerful
on so many levels.

00:23:41.820 --> 00:23:43.720
I mean, and just to hear the outcomes too,

00:23:43.720 --> 00:23:46.550
even though some of those
things weren't the focus,

00:23:46.550 --> 00:23:48.190
but just having that
five-minute pause time,

00:23:48.190 --> 00:23:51.100
how it had so many positive
effects in so many ways.

00:23:51.100 --> 00:23:51.933
I'm curious, you know,

00:23:51.933 --> 00:23:54.040
as I'm listening to you
talk through those things

00:23:54.040 --> 00:23:55.230
and because a lot of
what we've talked about

00:23:55.230 --> 00:23:57.680
has been on college and
university campuses,

00:23:57.680 --> 00:24:00.610
I'm curious how much there's
a resetting of expectations,

00:24:00.610 --> 00:24:02.130
'cause you even talked
about that from parents.

00:24:02.130 --> 00:24:04.610
But one thing that I've
also noticed is sometimes

00:24:04.610 --> 00:24:06.820
helping students reset
their own expectations

00:24:06.820 --> 00:24:09.020
because sometimes they're
setting them too high.

00:24:09.020 --> 00:24:09.853
- Yes.

00:24:09.853 --> 00:24:11.430
- Or something that pre-COVID,

00:24:11.430 --> 00:24:13.660
something that's not
attainable, falling short of it,

00:24:13.660 --> 00:24:16.560
feeling guilty and anxious
they didn't meet those goals

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:18.450
and still, and then
getting stuck in a cycle

00:24:18.450 --> 00:24:20.320
where they get further and
further away from those.

00:24:20.320 --> 00:24:21.700
Are there any aspects of that

00:24:21.700 --> 00:24:24.460
that you also see at
younger ages, as well,

00:24:24.460 --> 00:24:25.293
even as you've talked about

00:24:25.293 --> 00:24:26.700
the parents setting expectations,

00:24:26.700 --> 00:24:28.160
what about the kids themselves?

00:24:28.160 --> 00:24:29.970
- Oh, all across the board.

00:24:29.970 --> 00:24:31.070
Their expectations,

00:24:31.070 --> 00:24:33.400
they say fascinating of what do you need?

00:24:33.400 --> 00:24:34.460
I guess the first thing is,

00:24:34.460 --> 00:24:36.147
what do you need from your parents?

00:24:36.147 --> 00:24:36.980
- Mmm.

00:24:36.980 --> 00:24:38.640
- The number one thing
I get from the kids,

00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:40.067
oh, is I,

00:24:40.067 --> 00:24:41.430
"Would you please tell them

00:24:41.430 --> 00:24:43.520
to just say that you love me for who I am

00:24:43.520 --> 00:24:45.580
and don't quit asking me about the grade?

00:24:45.580 --> 00:24:49.070
'Cause we are so worried about
letting our parents down.

00:24:49.070 --> 00:24:50.730
I know they're worried about us,

00:24:50.730 --> 00:24:54.570
but we are really, really
scrambling to get it together."

00:24:54.570 --> 00:24:56.180
If you take one step more,

00:24:56.180 --> 00:24:57.920
you can teach goal-setting to kids.

00:24:57.920 --> 00:25:00.180
But goal-setting is, don't aim to here.

00:25:00.180 --> 00:25:02.230
A good coach of an Olympic
athlete never said,

00:25:02.230 --> 00:25:03.570
you're gonna get a gold medal tomorrow.

00:25:03.570 --> 00:25:04.403
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:25:04.403 --> 00:25:05.790
- Watch your time today,

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:07.650
and you teach them one step more

00:25:07.650 --> 00:25:09.730
and one step more and one step more.

00:25:09.730 --> 00:25:11.810
Or you have a child who's overwhelmed.

00:25:11.810 --> 00:25:13.790
Oh, my gosh, because of the anxiety.

00:25:13.790 --> 00:25:16.930
So what you do there is you
tell 'em to chunk the task

00:25:16.930 --> 00:25:19.470
and that is, let's look at it all.

00:25:19.470 --> 00:25:21.280
Okay. Don't go worried
about the whole thing.

00:25:21.280 --> 00:25:23.900
Let's do the first row or the second row,

00:25:23.900 --> 00:25:26.830
or is there one thing on that whole sheet

00:25:26.830 --> 00:25:28.380
that is your stumbler?

00:25:28.380 --> 00:25:29.213
- Hmm.

00:25:29.213 --> 00:25:32.350
- This example, I took piano as a kid

00:25:32.350 --> 00:25:36.000
and Mr. White drove me to
early retirement on the piano.

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:37.630
- You still remember his name. (laughs)

00:25:37.630 --> 00:25:39.320
- Oh, my God, Mr. White,

00:25:39.320 --> 00:25:42.080
if you made one mistake wherever you were,

00:25:42.080 --> 00:25:43.930
you had to start all over again.

00:25:43.930 --> 00:25:46.400
So what I would do was
only focus on the mistake.

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:50.700
My stress level was so high
that I started to hate piano.

00:25:50.700 --> 00:25:53.950
Bless Ms. Thompson, she
was my next teacher.

00:25:53.950 --> 00:25:56.570
And what she had is a
whole different approach.

00:25:56.570 --> 00:25:58.717
She'd identified my stumbler.

00:25:58.717 --> 00:26:01.100
"So one little thing that's
getting in your way, Michele.

00:26:01.100 --> 00:26:04.000
Let's just practice that little
bar over and over and over,

00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:04.890
now you're fine.

00:26:04.890 --> 00:26:05.917
Now start at the beginning."

00:26:05.917 --> 00:26:07.310
Well, now I'm relaxed

00:26:07.310 --> 00:26:08.786
and that's what a good coach does.

00:26:08.786 --> 00:26:09.619
- Yeah.

00:26:09.619 --> 00:26:10.900
- They never say, "You might as well quit.

00:26:10.900 --> 00:26:11.733
You can't do it right."

00:26:11.733 --> 00:26:13.410
No, let's look at the video.

00:26:13.410 --> 00:26:15.070
Oh, your foot's going the wrong way.

00:26:15.070 --> 00:26:17.650
Let's just practice your
foot going that way.

00:26:17.650 --> 00:26:20.710
See, the goal is to take all those little

00:26:20.710 --> 00:26:22.810
evidence-based strategies,

00:26:22.810 --> 00:26:23.643
not 'em all

00:26:23.643 --> 00:26:25.304
or your kid will never
let you read another book.

00:26:25.304 --> 00:26:26.137
- Yeah. (laughs)

00:26:26.137 --> 00:26:27.437
- One little thing at a time at time.

00:26:27.437 --> 00:26:28.830
What works for you

00:26:28.830 --> 00:26:32.410
until you can teach your
child to do it on their own.

00:26:32.410 --> 00:26:33.960
Because the one thing I have discovered

00:26:33.960 --> 00:26:36.770
about resilient children
is they have agency.

00:26:36.770 --> 00:26:38.640
They don't wait for us to do it.

00:26:38.640 --> 00:26:41.300
They finally figure out the strategies

00:26:41.300 --> 00:26:43.230
and that's what we've
gotta help our kids learn,

00:26:43.230 --> 00:26:45.680
is you've got it, but here's the strategy

00:26:45.680 --> 00:26:47.260
that'll help you get it.

00:26:47.260 --> 00:26:49.870
Practice and practice until
you can do it all your own,

00:26:49.870 --> 00:26:51.630
then they'll use it the
rest of their lives.

00:26:51.630 --> 00:26:52.730
- Yeah. That's so,

00:26:52.730 --> 00:26:54.170
I mean, so many practical pieces there

00:26:54.170 --> 00:26:56.710
that all seem like
they're a cultural shift.

00:26:56.710 --> 00:26:57.543
I mean, like you said,

00:26:57.543 --> 00:26:58.400
they're all evidence-based,

00:26:58.400 --> 00:27:01.602
but because of the
performance being so ingrained

00:27:01.602 --> 00:27:02.435
in our culture,

00:27:02.435 --> 00:27:04.944
I think it's that much
more difficult at times.

00:27:04.944 --> 00:27:07.200
- Well, I think the first step, you know,

00:27:07.200 --> 00:27:09.450
is you can't change your parenting

00:27:09.450 --> 00:27:11.560
or your teaching or anything,

00:27:11.560 --> 00:27:13.090
unless you understand the why.

00:27:13.090 --> 00:27:14.417
- Mmm, hmm mm.

00:27:14.417 --> 00:27:16.440
- And I always tell the school.

00:27:16.440 --> 00:27:19.150
I said, "The most wonderful
thing you could do is,

00:27:19.150 --> 00:27:21.680
and it won't take any more than a minute

00:27:21.680 --> 00:27:23.300
is give every kid a survey

00:27:23.300 --> 00:27:24.740
and just fill out three things

00:27:24.740 --> 00:27:27.320
of what's the one thing you
wish the teachers would,

00:27:27.320 --> 00:27:29.040
what do you appreciate about the teachers?

00:27:29.040 --> 00:27:30.860
What's the one thing you wished

00:27:30.860 --> 00:27:33.630
appreciate about your parents
or wish they would do?

00:27:33.630 --> 00:27:36.380
What's the one feeling that
you're having right now?

00:27:36.380 --> 00:27:40.090
And anxiety, stress, overwhelmed,

00:27:40.090 --> 00:27:41.628
always come up on the feeling.

00:27:41.628 --> 00:27:44.270
The one thing they appreciate
about the teachers,

00:27:44.270 --> 00:27:46.120
it makes them cry every time.

00:27:46.120 --> 00:27:48.310
I just did this with
Jacksonville, Florida,

00:27:48.310 --> 00:27:49.600
and they, the kids came up,

00:27:49.600 --> 00:27:50.653
these were teens.

00:27:51.590 --> 00:27:52.830
The lunch lady.

00:27:52.830 --> 00:27:54.860
I love her 'cause she sings all day

00:27:54.860 --> 00:27:57.420
and I walk into the lunch
room and she's singing.

00:27:57.420 --> 00:27:59.920
The custodian 'cause he says,
"Have a good one, spike,

00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:00.950
you're gonna do it."

00:28:00.950 --> 00:28:03.050
The teacher who waits for me at the door

00:28:03.050 --> 00:28:06.030
and says that simple, little things.

00:28:06.030 --> 00:28:07.950
And then I looked at the parents

00:28:07.950 --> 00:28:11.400
and I was frankly, I was like, oh my gosh,

00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:13.370
what's the one thing you
appreciate your parent

00:28:13.370 --> 00:28:14.480
or your family.

00:28:14.480 --> 00:28:16.840
I can't tell you how many times "My dog"

00:28:16.840 --> 00:28:17.976
came up on the list.

00:28:17.976 --> 00:28:19.050
- Wow.

00:28:19.050 --> 00:28:19.900
- My dog,

00:28:19.900 --> 00:28:21.930
that my mom drives me to school.

00:28:21.930 --> 00:28:24.000
It wasn't the things we said

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:28.160
that we've got to maybe have
some empathy for our children

00:28:28.160 --> 00:28:29.830
because they're really hurting right now.

00:28:29.830 --> 00:28:31.467
- Yeah, yeah.

00:28:31.467 --> 00:28:32.720
- And that surveys are so,

00:28:32.720 --> 00:28:34.770
I mean, even just the
naming it, as you mentioned,

00:28:34.770 --> 00:28:36.790
which goes back to, on the one hand,

00:28:36.790 --> 00:28:38.010
naming the things that are challenging

00:28:38.010 --> 00:28:39.510
and naming the things that are giving life

00:28:39.510 --> 00:28:40.343
and giving support.

00:28:40.343 --> 00:28:41.690
- Yes, yes.
- At the same time.

00:28:41.690 --> 00:28:42.523
So important.

00:28:43.467 --> 00:28:45.330
So I also, I mean, I'll throw
a little bit of an oddball,

00:28:45.330 --> 00:28:47.261
'cause this is a question
that I've been curious about

00:28:47.261 --> 00:28:48.850
for some time.

00:28:48.850 --> 00:28:50.960
Obviously, I know you focus
on the child development,

00:28:50.960 --> 00:28:52.040
but one thing that,

00:28:52.040 --> 00:28:52.873
again, just talking to kids

00:28:52.873 --> 00:28:53.920
that seems to come up,

00:28:53.920 --> 00:28:56.260
sometimes as I hear them kind
of talking about scenarios

00:28:56.260 --> 00:28:58.040
in the schools and again,

00:28:58.040 --> 00:29:00.610
the neuroscientist in me
automatically goes on to thinking,

00:29:00.610 --> 00:29:02.839
well, these teachers are spread very thin.

00:29:02.839 --> 00:29:03.672
- Yes.

00:29:03.672 --> 00:29:04.720
- And I'm not sure the teachers

00:29:04.720 --> 00:29:07.670
are actually attentive
to their own stressors.

00:29:07.670 --> 00:29:08.503
- Yes.

00:29:08.503 --> 00:29:09.336
- And irritants and things like that.

00:29:09.336 --> 00:29:10.930
So is that something that
comes up in conversation,

00:29:10.930 --> 00:29:13.020
even though I know you focus
on the child development,

00:29:13.020 --> 00:29:13.853
- No, no.

00:29:13.853 --> 00:29:16.310
- Awareness for the adults
who are in the class?

00:29:16.310 --> 00:29:18.780
- It's such a critical, critical comment.

00:29:18.780 --> 00:29:19.740
Because first of all,

00:29:19.740 --> 00:29:22.850
we are losing an enormous
amount of teachers

00:29:22.850 --> 00:29:23.930
who are retiring.

00:29:23.930 --> 00:29:25.900
If there's one thing
every one of us should do

00:29:25.900 --> 00:29:28.070
is write a thank you note to a teacher.

00:29:28.070 --> 00:29:31.030
'Cause the reason that
they got into teaching

00:29:31.030 --> 00:29:32.037
is because they love children

00:29:32.037 --> 00:29:33.880
and they wanna make a difference.

00:29:33.880 --> 00:29:35.530
Just writing 'em a thank you

00:29:35.530 --> 00:29:37.518
is one of the best ways to reduce burnout.

00:29:37.518 --> 00:29:40.700
Second of all is, at the end
of every one of my speeches,

00:29:40.700 --> 00:29:42.210
I spend 30 minutes talking about

00:29:42.210 --> 00:29:43.886
now, what are you gonna
do? Take care of yourself.

00:29:43.886 --> 00:29:46.830
What's your one go-to that works for you?

00:29:46.830 --> 00:29:50.550
I would suggest at schools that
you do more sunshine calls,

00:29:50.550 --> 00:29:53.330
just, just taking time for each other,

00:29:53.330 --> 00:29:56.610
collegiality, finding a support network.

00:29:56.610 --> 00:29:57.997
What is working for you?

00:29:57.997 --> 00:29:59.090
And in some cases,

00:29:59.090 --> 00:30:02.310
the counselor has a
before-school yoga group.

00:30:02.310 --> 00:30:04.390
That doesn't mean all
teachers are gonna do it.

00:30:04.390 --> 00:30:08.210
For some teachers, they
say they really love books.

00:30:08.210 --> 00:30:09.470
Bibliotherapy.

00:30:09.470 --> 00:30:11.340
Getting into the shoes of a character

00:30:11.340 --> 00:30:13.490
who's been through a tough time

00:30:13.490 --> 00:30:14.870
and coming out to the other side,

00:30:14.870 --> 00:30:15.703
reading the book,
"Educated", can be enormous.

00:30:15.703 --> 00:30:17.373
Malala's books can be extraordinary

00:30:17.373 --> 00:30:19.840
Malala's books can be extraordinary

00:30:19.840 --> 00:30:22.410
of here's a kid who
endured and got through.

00:30:22.410 --> 00:30:25.620
It helps the teacher realize
resilience is something

00:30:25.620 --> 00:30:27.059
that you're not born with.

00:30:27.059 --> 00:30:30.130
You can acquire it and
we can help our children

00:30:30.130 --> 00:30:31.270
and it's doable.

00:30:31.270 --> 00:30:34.350
But once they begin to
realize you can do it.

00:30:34.350 --> 00:30:36.230
I remember when I was
writing the book "Thrivers",

00:30:36.230 --> 00:30:39.620
each chapter has a real story
about somebody who endured

00:30:39.620 --> 00:30:42.757
and made it to extraordinary, tough time.

00:30:42.757 --> 00:30:46.440
And I remember when I was
writing the chapter on empathy,

00:30:46.440 --> 00:30:49.190
I was reading about Elizabeth Smart.

00:30:49.190 --> 00:30:51.960
Here is a girl who went
through nine months

00:30:51.960 --> 00:30:54.493
of absolute terror, kidnapped,

00:30:55.607 --> 00:30:57.640
a knife in her bedroom

00:30:57.640 --> 00:31:01.480
and then horrific horror of horrors.

00:31:01.480 --> 00:31:03.270
But the one chapter that just,

00:31:03.270 --> 00:31:05.330
it was the stop moment.

00:31:05.330 --> 00:31:06.890
How did she endure it?

00:31:06.890 --> 00:31:10.370
She said, "It was the
second day of captivity,

00:31:10.370 --> 00:31:11.940
when all of a sudden
I remembered something

00:31:11.940 --> 00:31:13.630
my mother said to me,

00:31:13.630 --> 00:31:16.480
she came crystal clear
into my head and she said,

00:31:16.480 --> 00:31:17.870
'Elizabeth, no matter what,

00:31:17.870 --> 00:31:19.790
remember, I will always love you,

00:31:19.790 --> 00:31:21.160
no matter whatever happens,

00:31:21.160 --> 00:31:22.360
I'm always there for you.'

00:31:22.360 --> 00:31:24.920
And I said, if my mother
is always going to love me

00:31:24.920 --> 00:31:27.210
for who I am, I'm gonna
be able to get through it.

00:31:27.210 --> 00:31:29.810
She says, that gave me the
fortitude to keep going.

00:31:29.810 --> 00:31:30.643
- Wow.

00:31:31.530 --> 00:31:32.740
Wow.

00:31:32.740 --> 00:31:34.060
That's so powerful.

00:31:34.060 --> 00:31:34.893
- Yeah.

00:31:34.893 --> 00:31:37.500
So I think we forget those ordinary things

00:31:37.500 --> 00:31:40.150
and it's like go home and hug your kid,

00:31:40.150 --> 00:31:42.590
but tell 'em, "I'm here for
you no matter what happens.

00:31:42.590 --> 00:31:44.180
I love you for who you are."

00:31:44.180 --> 00:31:47.635
- Yeah. That's so, so easy
for us to slip outta that.

00:31:47.635 --> 00:31:49.100
And I think that ties everything together,

00:31:49.100 --> 00:31:49.933
what you've been talking about,

00:31:49.933 --> 00:31:52.210
both from the kids and
from our expectations

00:31:52.210 --> 00:31:55.423
as we all kind of get
spread thin in those places.

00:31:56.601 --> 00:31:57.434
So, wow.

00:31:57.434 --> 00:31:58.267
Just, I mean,

00:31:58.267 --> 00:31:59.450
I just feel like you just
need just to pause there

00:31:59.450 --> 00:32:00.630
and just really take that in.

00:32:00.630 --> 00:32:01.880
'Cause I think that comment,

00:32:01.880 --> 00:32:03.980
even though itself can go such a long way,

00:32:04.819 --> 00:32:06.470
but the other thing which really stand up,

00:32:06.470 --> 00:32:07.303
stands out from what you're talking about

00:32:07.303 --> 00:32:09.190
is just the learning that happens.

00:32:09.190 --> 00:32:10.273
The learning of the resilience.

00:32:10.273 --> 00:32:13.170
Now you've talked about
that with empathy as well.

00:32:13.170 --> 00:32:14.980
So I'd also wanna get to your new book,

00:32:14.980 --> 00:32:17.800
but maybe to park on this
empathy piece for a little bit

00:32:17.800 --> 00:32:19.330
and just have you elaborate

00:32:19.330 --> 00:32:22.500
on what you've learned
about really teaching that

00:32:22.500 --> 00:32:24.190
and how that can be developed

00:32:24.190 --> 00:32:26.310
and kind of stick for the long term,

00:32:26.310 --> 00:32:27.250
what are some of the principles

00:32:27.250 --> 00:32:28.600
that have come up in your work?

00:32:28.600 --> 00:32:30.453
- I think it was dealing with,

00:32:31.330 --> 00:32:32.580
well, it was once again,

00:32:32.580 --> 00:32:34.450
a moment that just changed my life.

00:32:34.450 --> 00:32:37.931
It was sitting on a bench in
the Cambodian killing fields.

00:32:37.931 --> 00:32:38.860
- [Addy] Mmm.

00:32:38.860 --> 00:32:41.360
- And it was, I had a an,

00:32:41.360 --> 00:32:43.670
because there, in front of me was a tree

00:32:43.670 --> 00:32:45.450
because their in front of me was a tree

00:32:45.450 --> 00:32:46.810
with these little ribbons on it.

00:32:46.810 --> 00:32:49.070
And each ribbon stood for a child

00:32:49.070 --> 00:32:51.060
who had been slaughtered on that tree

00:32:51.060 --> 00:32:52.890
because they were crying.

00:32:52.890 --> 00:32:57.830
Next to me was a monolith of
five stories high of skulls.

00:32:57.830 --> 00:33:01.057
And that was, that President
was a former teacher

00:33:01.057 --> 00:33:05.110
And that was, that president
was a former teacher

00:33:05.110 --> 00:33:07.172
who set the evil, so that's doable.

00:33:07.172 --> 00:33:08.870
I couldn't believe it.

00:33:08.870 --> 00:33:11.350
I happened to walk off the killing fields

00:33:11.350 --> 00:33:14.650
and there was a little teeny table

00:33:14.650 --> 00:33:16.450
that was selling a few books

00:33:16.450 --> 00:33:18.620
and there was my, oh my gosh,

00:33:18.620 --> 00:33:20.460
but I picked it up.

00:33:20.460 --> 00:33:21.757
It was by Samuel Oliner,

00:33:21.757 --> 00:33:24.230
one of the most amazing
social psychologists.

00:33:24.230 --> 00:33:26.570
His family had been slaughtered in Germany

00:33:26.570 --> 00:33:27.543
during the Holocaust.

00:33:27.543 --> 00:33:29.590
He had a perfect woman,

00:33:29.590 --> 00:33:33.030
just didn't know anyone or him.

00:33:33.030 --> 00:33:35.260
Samuel was 12 at the time.

00:33:35.260 --> 00:33:36.190
Her name was Bowinda.

00:33:36.190 --> 00:33:37.673
She said, "Samuel, come in here.

00:33:37.673 --> 00:33:40.440
I need to save you, run
as fast as you can."

00:33:40.440 --> 00:33:43.030
His family went off to a death camp.

00:33:43.030 --> 00:33:44.240
She saved his life,

00:33:44.240 --> 00:33:46.080
and now the entire rest of his life,

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:49.500
he's trying to figure out what
makes Bowinda's of the world?

00:33:49.500 --> 00:33:52.360
Not the evil, what flips the other side.

00:33:52.360 --> 00:33:54.320
And what he did was extraordinary.

00:33:54.320 --> 00:33:57.240
He's interviewed hundreds of rescuers,

00:33:57.240 --> 00:33:59.320
Christians during World War II,

00:33:59.320 --> 00:34:02.517
who risked their life
to save Jewish people.

00:34:02.517 --> 00:34:03.980
"Why?" he said,

00:34:03.980 --> 00:34:06.610
and the commonality that each
one of them were three things,

00:34:06.610 --> 00:34:08.800
I call 'em the three Es.

00:34:08.800 --> 00:34:11.120
Every one of them said
it was how I was raised.

00:34:11.120 --> 00:34:13.030
Went, "Whoa, how were you raised."

00:34:13.030 --> 00:34:14.980
The first thing was example.

00:34:14.980 --> 00:34:16.980
They saw my dad or I saw my mom.

00:34:16.980 --> 00:34:20.638
She always really was the
example of empathy or kindness.

00:34:20.638 --> 00:34:23.360
The second thing was
expectations in our family.

00:34:23.360 --> 00:34:24.680
You were expected to be kind,

00:34:24.680 --> 00:34:26.350
you were expected to do good.

00:34:26.350 --> 00:34:28.260
The third one was experiences.

00:34:28.260 --> 00:34:31.790
My dad would walk it, my mom would say it,

00:34:31.790 --> 00:34:33.320
but then we did experiences.

00:34:33.320 --> 00:34:36.760
So we would deliver
overcoats to a needy family

00:34:36.760 --> 00:34:39.320
or we'd bake cookies to
the neighbor next door.

00:34:39.320 --> 00:34:42.330
Those three things
started to build empathy.

00:34:42.330 --> 00:34:45.423
So each person said, I saw
myself as a caring person.

00:34:45.423 --> 00:34:47.140
What the parents were doing

00:34:47.140 --> 00:34:49.520
was changing the child's mindset.

00:34:49.520 --> 00:34:51.880
So now they see themself
as a caring person.

00:34:51.880 --> 00:34:53.380
You act how you see yourself to be.

00:34:53.380 --> 00:34:55.230
And I think what we've done is

00:34:55.230 --> 00:34:58.090
we put so much of our mindset into the GPA

00:34:58.090 --> 00:34:59.210
and the test score

00:34:59.210 --> 00:35:01.657
that when the kid comes
home, we rarely say,

00:35:01.657 --> 00:35:03.030
"What good thing did you do?"

00:35:03.030 --> 00:35:05.930
As opposed to, "What grade did you get?"

00:35:05.930 --> 00:35:07.801
It doesn't mean you're gonna stop asking.

00:35:07.801 --> 00:35:09.580
"Are you studying your hardest?"

00:35:09.580 --> 00:35:12.580
But it also means, this
was I expect you to do.

00:35:12.580 --> 00:35:15.820
The lesson from it all is that
empathy can be cultivated.

00:35:15.820 --> 00:35:18.610
And the research now is
clear that our, we are,

00:35:18.610 --> 00:35:20.408
our children are hardwired for it.

00:35:20.408 --> 00:35:23.950
But unless we really make
it a little more intentional

00:35:23.950 --> 00:35:26.170
in today's world, in particular,

00:35:26.170 --> 00:35:27.750
it's gonna lie dormant.

00:35:27.750 --> 00:35:29.000
And I, I swear,

00:35:29.000 --> 00:35:32.500
after, especially after two
years of physical distancing

00:35:32.500 --> 00:35:35.740
and looking at a screen,
as opposed to face-to-face,

00:35:35.740 --> 00:35:39.020
because the pathway to
empathy is looking at someone

00:35:39.020 --> 00:35:40.910
and going, "How would she feel?

00:35:40.910 --> 00:35:42.730
How do I feel?"

00:35:42.730 --> 00:35:46.330
That emotional literacy
piece starts the trajectory

00:35:46.330 --> 00:35:48.473
towards I'm gonna reach out and help.

00:35:49.320 --> 00:35:51.720
So those are the pieces you learned.

00:35:51.720 --> 00:35:52.873
And that's why wrote "UnSelfie".

00:35:52.873 --> 00:35:55.310
I went, okay, if this is doable,

00:35:55.310 --> 00:35:57.330
then what are the habits
that we can start teaching?

00:35:57.330 --> 00:35:58.930
And let's make it easy.

00:35:58.930 --> 00:36:01.080
And I, one of the most critical ones

00:36:01.080 --> 00:36:04.130
is besides instilling in your
child, the caring mindset,

00:36:04.130 --> 00:36:08.037
we need to talk feelings far
more naturally to our kids.

00:36:08.037 --> 00:36:10.510
We can watch "Inside
Out" with our older kids.

00:36:10.510 --> 00:36:12.760
We can say, "How are you doing today?"

00:36:12.760 --> 00:36:14.700
We can say, "Sit here
and watch dad's face.

00:36:14.700 --> 00:36:15.780
And when he walks in

00:36:15.780 --> 00:36:16.900
and you'll know if this is a good time

00:36:16.900 --> 00:36:18.550
to ask him for the allowance or not."

00:36:18.550 --> 00:36:21.353
Can be extremely wonderful moments,

00:36:21.353 --> 00:36:23.040
but you can also, for some children,

00:36:23.040 --> 00:36:25.998
they're a little nervous
about saying the emotion.

00:36:25.998 --> 00:36:28.670
So you can do what an ER doctor says.

00:36:28.670 --> 00:36:30.660
And he always says, how bad's the pain?

00:36:30.660 --> 00:36:32.110
Show me with your fingers.

00:36:32.110 --> 00:36:33.610
A zero is you could be asleep,

00:36:33.610 --> 00:36:35.640
it's so, you're so relaxed.

00:36:35.640 --> 00:36:37.620
A seven, you're about ready to explode

00:36:37.620 --> 00:36:39.410
because the pain is so bad,

00:36:39.410 --> 00:36:40.960
but you can do that with your children.

00:36:40.960 --> 00:36:43.597
Your children can give
you, "I'm at a three, mom".

00:36:43.597 --> 00:36:44.950
"Thank you for telling me, sweetie.

00:36:44.950 --> 00:36:47.118
Let's figure out how to
get it to a two or a one."

00:36:47.118 --> 00:36:48.603
You know?
- [Addy] Yeah.

00:36:49.480 --> 00:36:50.860
- It's that empathy piece

00:36:50.860 --> 00:36:53.230
of us trying to empathize with our kids,

00:36:53.230 --> 00:36:54.900
is the starter point.

00:36:54.900 --> 00:36:58.190
Then we start helping them learn emotions.

00:36:58.190 --> 00:37:00.510
We start helping them
not see it in themselves,

00:37:00.510 --> 00:37:02.300
but how would you feel
if that happened to you?

00:37:02.300 --> 00:37:04.240
What is she doing in order to feel better?

00:37:04.240 --> 00:37:05.750
What can you do?

00:37:05.750 --> 00:37:08.660
You watch TV and it's just gloriously,

00:37:08.660 --> 00:37:11.070
oh, just horrific stuff.

00:37:11.070 --> 00:37:13.290
But you can also say,
what can we do to help?

00:37:13.290 --> 00:37:14.600
- Mm hmm.

00:37:14.600 --> 00:37:16.010
- Yeah. That's a good idea.

00:37:16.010 --> 00:37:18.070
I know you're really upset by that.

00:37:18.070 --> 00:37:19.570
But let's go collect some,

00:37:19.570 --> 00:37:22.570
that's gonna keep a box by your back door.

00:37:22.570 --> 00:37:24.710
It's a charity box for your family.

00:37:24.710 --> 00:37:27.420
And it's for gently used toys and books

00:37:27.420 --> 00:37:29.680
and whatever, backpacks,

00:37:29.680 --> 00:37:31.420
and each time it's filled,

00:37:31.420 --> 00:37:34.320
your family, not you, mom, dad,

00:37:34.320 --> 00:37:38.090
your family delivers
it to someone in need.

00:37:38.090 --> 00:37:40.690
Every child said it was the
look in the other person's eye

00:37:40.690 --> 00:37:43.239
that said, "I gotta keep doing this."

00:37:43.239 --> 00:37:44.280
- Mmm, mm.

00:37:44.280 --> 00:37:45.383
That's so, so well said.

00:37:45.383 --> 00:37:47.590
I mean, I love so many pieces of that.

00:37:47.590 --> 00:37:49.086
Just the three Es that you shared too.

00:37:49.086 --> 00:37:51.200
I mean, I've been
thinking about the example

00:37:51.200 --> 00:37:52.970
and the experience together,

00:37:52.970 --> 00:37:54.750
because as you're talking about parents

00:37:54.750 --> 00:37:56.240
really tuning in with their children,

00:37:56.240 --> 00:37:58.300
but then also having time to say,

00:37:58.300 --> 00:38:00.940
as parents, well, this is what
I'm feeling in this moment.

00:38:00.940 --> 00:38:04.250
And to have the kids also
learn from that example.

00:38:04.250 --> 00:38:05.083
And again, you know,

00:38:05.083 --> 00:38:07.270
I keep kinda moving back
to the college sphere

00:38:07.270 --> 00:38:08.530
because that's a space that I live in,

00:38:08.530 --> 00:38:11.190
but I've even seen that on
campus in a lot of ways, too,

00:38:11.190 --> 00:38:12.900
with a lot of the social justice issues

