and then tie into so many
of those practical outcomes

00:38:36.780 --> 00:38:38.370
that you're talking about, as well.

00:38:38.370 --> 00:38:40.900
- Well, I think the
trajectory from me to we,

00:38:40.900 --> 00:38:42.437
and that's where we're aiming for.

00:38:42.437 --> 00:38:45.000
Oh, I hope we're aiming
for us as a society

00:38:45.000 --> 00:38:46.830
is to listen to other people's stories,

00:38:46.830 --> 00:38:49.170
to realize how much we
have in common with them.

00:38:49.170 --> 00:38:50.850
When a child says he's different,

00:38:50.850 --> 00:38:52.523
now let's find out how we're the same.

00:38:52.523 --> 00:38:55.650
It's too often that sped apart

00:38:55.650 --> 00:38:58.150
and we are becoming so
divisive as a group.

00:38:58.150 --> 00:39:00.070
We can't, we've gotta figure out,

00:39:00.070 --> 00:39:02.410
look at the kind of books
you're reading to your children,

00:39:02.410 --> 00:39:04.260
make sure that you're
reading different genders,

00:39:04.260 --> 00:39:05.860
different races, different incomes,

00:39:05.860 --> 00:39:06.940
different possibilities.

00:39:06.940 --> 00:39:09.040
Because books,

00:39:09.040 --> 00:39:10.920
books, literary fiction

00:39:10.920 --> 00:39:12.950
are known to be able to
open up your empathy.

00:39:12.950 --> 00:39:14.440
Ah, okay.

00:39:14.440 --> 00:39:17.370
They also help you in terms of learning,

00:39:17.370 --> 00:39:19.410
but you find the right book,

00:39:19.410 --> 00:39:21.610
that's why "Wonder" is one
of the most popular books

00:39:21.610 --> 00:39:22.803
for children right now.
- Yeah.

00:39:22.803 --> 00:39:25.352
- Because they feel with that character.

00:39:25.352 --> 00:39:26.185
- Yeah.

00:39:26.185 --> 00:39:27.347
- I ask middle school kids,

00:39:27.347 --> 00:39:28.323
"What's the one book

00:39:28.323 --> 00:39:31.950
that teachers should always,
always, always help kids read?"

00:39:31.950 --> 00:39:34.920
And I'm blown away that
they say "The Outsiders".

00:39:34.920 --> 00:39:36.854
I went, "That's 50 years old. Why?"

00:39:36.854 --> 00:39:38.560
I love their answer

00:39:38.560 --> 00:39:39.690
because we've gotta understand

00:39:39.690 --> 00:39:41.304
what it feels like to be excluded.

00:39:41.304 --> 00:39:42.230
- Mmm.

00:39:42.230 --> 00:39:43.897
- How glorious to have that as an answer?

00:39:43.897 --> 00:39:46.370
And that means that you're exposing kids

00:39:46.370 --> 00:39:49.170
and then you're doing something
wonderful, like you did,

00:39:49.170 --> 00:39:50.920
having the space to be
able to talk about it,

00:39:50.920 --> 00:39:53.150
have those dialogues

00:39:53.150 --> 00:39:55.453
where a kid knows it's okay to open up

00:39:55.453 --> 00:39:57.200
and to be assertive

00:39:57.200 --> 00:39:59.330
because that's another
trait of resilient children,

00:39:59.330 --> 00:40:00.453
they have voice.

00:40:02.560 --> 00:40:03.393
- Hmm, hmm.

00:40:03.393 --> 00:40:04.477
Yeah. So important.

00:40:04.477 --> 00:40:06.504
And I love, I mean, just
as we're talking through,

00:40:06.504 --> 00:40:07.337
this is one of those,

00:40:07.337 --> 00:40:08.264
I'm definitely gonna have
to go back and listen to,

00:40:08.264 --> 00:40:10.250
'cause I think you're bringing
so many things to life.

00:40:10.250 --> 00:40:12.410
And like you said, there's
so much evidence for it.

00:40:12.410 --> 00:40:14.280
But to just to hear it in this context

00:40:14.280 --> 00:40:16.310
and be reminded of it,
I think is so important.

00:40:16.310 --> 00:40:17.500
There's so many practical pieces

00:40:17.500 --> 00:40:18.785
that we can pull out of it.

00:40:18.785 --> 00:40:19.690
- Thank you.

00:40:19.690 --> 00:40:20.820
Well, that was the goal.

00:40:20.820 --> 00:40:24.170
The goal was really after
listening to my dad,

00:40:24.170 --> 00:40:26.880
I saw two huge needs and I think it was,

00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:30.480
again, I started to do
focus groups with teens

00:40:30.480 --> 00:40:33.410
and I never realized
prior to the pandemic,

00:40:33.410 --> 00:40:34.753
how struggling,

00:40:34.753 --> 00:40:36.760
they were struggling, they were hurting,

00:40:36.760 --> 00:40:38.040
their anxiety, their stress,

00:40:38.040 --> 00:40:40.130
even though were loved to death,

00:40:40.130 --> 00:40:42.000
their opportunities were endless,

00:40:42.000 --> 00:40:44.230
but something was really
amiss with the kids.

00:40:44.230 --> 00:40:46.150
And then the pandemic hit.

00:40:46.150 --> 00:40:48.960
Well, a crisis only amplifies
a preexisting issue.

00:40:48.960 --> 00:40:49.997
And therefore the CDC

00:40:49.997 --> 00:40:53.040
and the Surgeon General's
reports on mental health needs

00:40:53.040 --> 00:40:54.260
are critical.

00:40:54.260 --> 00:40:55.370
But it also means,

00:40:55.370 --> 00:40:57.800
hey folks, we gotta reset
what we're doing here.

00:40:57.800 --> 00:40:59.990
We have to reset what we're thinking.

00:40:59.990 --> 00:41:01.370
And this is,

00:41:01.370 --> 00:41:03.440
when I wrote the last one, "Thrivers",

00:41:03.440 --> 00:41:05.780
was, empathy was what I did

00:41:05.780 --> 00:41:09.850
was to choose the seven most
highly correlated strengths

00:41:09.850 --> 00:41:12.420
that research and all the
work on resilience says

00:41:12.420 --> 00:41:13.610
are teachable,

00:41:13.610 --> 00:41:16.030
that will impact mental
health, resilience,

00:41:16.030 --> 00:41:17.660
and peak performance.

00:41:17.660 --> 00:41:20.340
Of course, number two
on the list was empathy.

00:41:20.340 --> 00:41:23.167
'Cause I don't think you can
go anywhere without empathy.

00:41:23.167 --> 00:41:25.437
But the first on the list was confidence.

00:41:25.437 --> 00:41:27.700
The child needs to know who they are,

00:41:27.700 --> 00:41:30.610
what their passion is that
we talked about earlier.

00:41:30.610 --> 00:41:32.703
And we've driven that passion out of it.

00:41:32.703 --> 00:41:33.837
Many kids said,

00:41:33.837 --> 00:41:37.200
"We're being driven to be
what my parents want me to be,

00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:38.880
not who I am."

00:41:38.880 --> 00:41:40.440
I think that's so essential.

00:41:40.440 --> 00:41:43.330
The third one we already talked
about too, was self control.

00:41:43.330 --> 00:41:46.140
Unless you can figure out how
to put the break on impulses

00:41:46.140 --> 00:41:49.270
and do that one-two breath
or whatever works for you.

00:41:49.270 --> 00:41:51.570
Fourth is integrity.

00:41:51.570 --> 00:41:53.670
That would be what
Samuel Oliner discovered,

00:41:53.670 --> 00:41:55.410
those parents knew exactly

00:41:55.410 --> 00:41:57.713
what the kind of kid they wanted
to turn out of that house.

00:41:57.713 --> 00:41:59.840
In this house, we're gonna be kind,

00:41:59.840 --> 00:42:00.673
we're gonna be caring,

00:42:00.673 --> 00:42:02.760
we're gonna be socially responsible.

00:42:02.760 --> 00:42:04.060
Empathy and social responsible

00:42:04.060 --> 00:42:07.520
were the two highest
correlated values of a home

00:42:07.520 --> 00:42:09.340
that raises a kid who's altruist.

00:42:09.340 --> 00:42:10.290
- Wow.

00:42:10.290 --> 00:42:12.845
- But they kept going
that over and over again.

00:42:12.845 --> 00:42:15.860
And then the fifth one is curiosity.

00:42:15.860 --> 00:42:17.960
We've helicoptered our kids to death.

00:42:17.960 --> 00:42:19.850
We've solved every problem for them.

00:42:19.850 --> 00:42:21.490
There goes the agency.

00:42:21.490 --> 00:42:24.070
So curiosity is helping
the kid be creative

00:42:24.070 --> 00:42:26.230
and open to ideas and possibilities.

00:42:26.230 --> 00:42:27.570
So when the challenge comes,

00:42:27.570 --> 00:42:28.660
you're not dumbfounded.

00:42:28.660 --> 00:42:31.325
You figure out what am I gonna do instead.

00:42:31.325 --> 00:42:33.720
Mom's not gonna help me this time.

00:42:33.720 --> 00:42:35.576
She's gonna teach me how to brainstorm.

00:42:35.576 --> 00:42:37.170
What's the problem?

00:42:37.170 --> 00:42:38.710
What's one thing you could
have done differently

00:42:38.710 --> 00:42:40.291
in another thing?

00:42:40.291 --> 00:42:42.350
Sixth is perseverance.

00:42:42.350 --> 00:42:44.370
Now you're gonna keep on going

00:42:44.370 --> 00:42:46.370
because you've got all
these other little skills

00:42:46.370 --> 00:42:47.460
along the way,

00:42:47.460 --> 00:42:49.030
and you're gonna realize your effort

00:42:49.030 --> 00:42:50.540
is what gets you moving,

00:42:50.540 --> 00:42:53.920
not your GPA or your zip code.

00:42:53.920 --> 00:42:56.280
And the final one is optimism and hope.

00:42:56.280 --> 00:42:58.080
If we don't instill that in a child,

00:42:59.070 --> 00:43:00.760
particularly in today's world

00:43:00.760 --> 00:43:02.760
and the images that they're seeing.

00:43:02.760 --> 00:43:03.680
After a while,

00:43:03.680 --> 00:43:05.580
a lot of 'em are gonna
start raising a white flag

00:43:05.580 --> 00:43:07.480
of I surrender, why should I do it?

00:43:07.480 --> 00:43:08.500
And I give up,

00:43:08.500 --> 00:43:10.170
that's not the kind of child we want.

00:43:10.170 --> 00:43:14.320
We want a strong kid who knows
the world is gonna be okay.

00:43:14.320 --> 00:43:17.630
And there's things that
you can do to make it okay.

00:43:17.630 --> 00:43:20.100
- Yeah, and again, just
backed up by all the research

00:43:20.100 --> 00:43:21.210
and tying that full circle,

00:43:21.210 --> 00:43:22.940
especially ending with that hope,

00:43:22.940 --> 00:43:24.960
as you mentioned before, too.

00:43:24.960 --> 00:43:25.793
I mean, there's so many,

00:43:25.793 --> 00:43:27.100
so many good things that you're doing.

